<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
                   xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
                   http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-3.5.xsd">

    <!--
      P0A2 — NOT-NULL enforcement for the tenant_id columns that 20260910003 could NOT
      enforce on MSSP deployments, plus the UBA columns added by 20260910004.

      This is the "later changeset AFTER that job completes" referenced in the comment of
      20260910003_tenant_backfill_notnull.xml. It is the FINAL step of the tenant-isolation
      backfill programme and is deliberately kept separate because it is irreversible:
      once tenant_id is NOT NULL, a row that could not be attributed to a tenant can no
      longer be inserted, and any surviving legacy NULL row would break this migration.

      HOW EACH CHANGESET IS GATED — the guard is the DATA STATE, not the deployment shape.
      Every changeset below runs its addNotNullConstraint ONLY when a sqlCheck confirms the
      column already has ZERO NULLs; otherwise the preCondition fails and the changeset is
      MARK_RAN (no-op, recorded as applied). This fails SAFE:

        * If the operator has NOT yet run the app-level backfill job
          (POST /api/ha-tenant-backfill, the A2-B TenantBackfillService), or the job left
          orphaned rows it could not resolve, some tenant_id values are still NULL. The
          sqlCheck then returns a non-zero count, the preCondition fails, and NOT-NULL is
          NOT applied — the migration cannot break a partially-backfilled table.

        * Once the operator has run the job AND confirmed the reported remainingNull /
          orphan counts are zero for a table, the next deploy's Liquibase run finds zero
          NULLs, the preCondition passes, and NOT-NULL is enforced.

      So enforcement is self-sequencing: it lands automatically on the first deploy after
      the data is clean, and quietly waits (MARK_RAN) on every deploy before that. No
      operator flag, profile, or manual changeset edit is required.

      NOTE on the UBA tables: A2-B's TenantBackfillService can resolve legacy UBA rows on
      SINGLE-TENANT deployments (tenant_id = 0), but on MSSP it deliberately does NOT
      host-match UBA rows (entity_id is a user/ip/host value, not an agent identifier — see
      TenantBackfillService.UBA_TABLES). MSSP legacy UBA rows therefore remain NULL until a
      UBA-specific migration resolves them from the originating alert's tenant. The
      zero-null gate below encodes exactly that: UBA NOT-NULL will NOT be enforced on an
      MSSP deployment that still has unresolved legacy UBA rows, and WILL be enforced on
      single-tenant (and on MSSP once those rows are resolved). No wrong tenant is ever
      stamped by this changeset — it only enforces the constraint, it does not backfill.
    -->

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- MSSP EDR / response-rule tables (single-tenant done by -003)  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->

    <!-- hive_edr_event -->
    <changeSet id="20260910005-1" author="hivearmor">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <and>
                <columnExists tableName="hive_edr_event" columnName="tenant_id"/>
                <sqlCheck expectedResult="0">
                    SELECT COUNT(*) FROM hive_edr_event WHERE tenant_id IS NULL
                </sqlCheck>
            </and>
        </preConditions>
        <addNotNullConstraint tableName="hive_edr_event" columnName="tenant_id"
                              columnDataType="BIGINT"/>
    </changeSet>

